<article class="env-lemma" id="081E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/081E">Lemma <span data-tag="081E">32.8.11</span>.</a> Notation and assumptions as in Situation <a href="/tag/081D" data-tag="081D">32.8.1</a>. If </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is an isomorphism, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f_0$ is locally of finite presentation, 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then $f_ i$ is an isomorphism for some $i \geq 0$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemmas <a href="/tag/07RP" data-tag="07RP">32.8.10</a> and <a href="/tag/01ZP" data-tag="01ZP">32.8.5</a> we can find an $i$ such that $f_ i$ is flat and a closed immersion. Then $f_ i$ identifies $X_ i$ with an open and closed subscheme of $Y_ i$, see Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/0819" data-tag="0819">29.27.2</a>. By assumption the image of $Y \to Y_ i$ maps into $f_ i(X_ i)$. Thus by Lemma <a href="/tag/05F4" data-tag="05F4">32.4.10</a> we find that $Y_{i'}$ maps into $f_ i(X_ i)$ for some $i' \geq i$. It follows that $X_{i'} \to Y_{i'}$ is surjective and we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>